	abstract = {With the growing integration of the Internet of Things (IoT) into power systems, the concept of the Energy Internet (EI) has emerged, realizing the vision of sharing energy like data. This paradigm enables efficient monitoring and seamless interconnection between traditional smart grids and renewable energy resources, but it also introduces critical security challenges, particularly with the advent of quantum computing. To address these challenges, researchers have proposed a range of security solutions. In this context, this study presents the first systematic survey of EI security approaches, covering works published between 2017 and 2025. The reviewed works were analyzed and classified into four categories: traditional cryptography, post-quantum and quantum cryptography, blockchain, and hybrid solutions. The findings reveal that blockchain (29.41%), hybrid (29.41%), and traditional cryptography (23.52%) dominate the literature, while post-quantum and quantum cryptography (17.64%) remain underexplored despite their long-term importance. Furthermore, most existing works focus on authentication, confidentiality, privacy, and integrity, while issues such as transparency, authorization, and quantum resilience are largely neglected. Finally, the paper discusses open challenges and future directions, emphasizing the need for scalable, energy-efficient, and quantum-resistant security frameworks for the EI. Β© 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
