	affiliations = {Eskisehir Ozmangazi University, Department of Software Engineering, Eskisehir, Turkey},
	abstract = {In computer science and engineering, set membership tests are of critical importance in numerous areas, such as databases and verification systems. Traditional search algorithms become inefficient in terms of time and memory as the set size increases. To overcome these limitations, cryptographic accumulators, which are specialized data structures that perform set membership tests efficiently, have been proposed. Accumulators compress the values of a large set into a small value, offering the ability to effectively prove the existence of set elements. Cryptographic accumulators play an effective role in solving various problems such as privacy-preserving data sharing, verification of data integrity, and particularly authentication in areas like identity verification systems, blockchain, and pattern matching. Existing cryptographic accumulators are based on computationally challenging mathematical problems such as factoring large integers or discrete logarithms. However, with the development of quantum computers, the computational security of these algorithms has been compromised. In this study, a hash tree-based universal accumulator scheme was developed using a code-based cryptographic accumulator resistant to quantum attacks. This new scheme aims to ensure data security in the post-quantum era.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
