	abstract = {We present AmarVote, a secure e-voting system that enhances modern electronic voting infrastructure with selective post-quantum cryptographic protection. AmarVote is built on Microsoft's open-source ElectionGuard library which provides homomorphic encryption for ballot privacy and cryptographic verifiability. ElectionGuard achieves end-To-end verifiability using an ElGamal-based cryptosystem, but leaves guardian private keys and threshold polynomials-critical components of threshold cryptography used by election officials to decrypt results-unprotected against future quantum attacks. Since these credentials safeguard election integrity, their compromise would pose significant risks. AmarVote strengthens security by employing ML-KEM-1024 to encrypt guardian private keys and polynomials which provides quantum-resistant protection while preserving compatibility with ElectionGuard's encryption and homomorphic tallying. It also introduces a prototype blockchain-based auditing layer using Ganache for tamper-evident ballot tracking. We evaluate AmarVote performance through deployment in cloud infrastructure (Azure and VMware), scaling behavior with increasing parameters.  Β© 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
