	abstract = {The Internet of Things (IoT) and the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) are undergoing rapid transformation, bringing new cybersecurity problems to the field. Due to the epidemic rise of IoT devices' integration with critical infrastructure, it becomes important to speed up the development of new security frameworks to detect forthcoming threats. This chapter assesses the future trends and IoT security technologies, highlighting Zero-Trust Architecture's (ZTA) critical role in preventing cyber threats. ZTA will transform existing frameworks as a security blueprint with its authentication, access control, and system segmentation principles. Hence, the security of IoT is improved by combining blockchain technology for secure transactions and Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) features to find threats. The development of quantum computing makes it necessary for the use of quantum-resistant cryptography to secure the communication of IoT devices now possible. Autonomous security agents and edge intelligence with AI-driven real-time solutions to find and react simultaneously to threats are the basis of future IoT security. According to the document, strong security protocols are still needed, but they must adapt as circumstances change to protect the IoT backdrop against intensifying complexity and new threats. Β© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
