	abstract = {The Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is a symmetric encryption algorithm advanced through Joan Daemen and Vincent Rijmen, adopted as desired via NIST in 2001. It replaced the Data Encryption Standard (DES) to cope with vulnerabilities in cryptographic security. AES is based mostly on the Rijndael cipher and operates on constant 128-bit block sizes, helping key lengths of 128, 192, and 256 bits. It makes use of a sequence of roundsβ€”SubBytes, ShiftRows, MixColumns, and AddRoundKeyβ€”to ensure strong encryption. The wide style of rounds will increase with a key period, balancing performance and protection. AES is extensively used for protecting touchy facts in charge structures, cloud garages, IoT, and wi-fi communications because of its velocity, performance, and resistance to assaults. AES advances include quantum-resistant algorithms, privacy-maintaining computations for AI, and strong statistics sharing in blockchain structures. These improvements make sure AES remains a cornerstone of cryptography, evolving alongside technological enhancements to strong records in an increasing number of complex environments. Β© Grenze Scientific Society, 2025.},
