	journal = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Multi-Agent Systems for Collaborative Intelligence, ICMSCI 2025},
	pages = {161 - 165},
	doi = {10.1109/ICMSCI62561.2025.10893968},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105000538539?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Srm Institute of Science and Technology, Computing Technologies, Chennai, India},
	abstract = {In this ever-evolving field of studies, clinical trials demand efficiency and data security. The project will investigate the integration of blockchain, in combination with quantum technology in solving challenges faced by current clinical trials. This would help in streamlining trial management to guarantee that data is transparent, traceable, and tamper-proof. Data tampering, delays in recruitment, and inefficient data systems contribute to slow trials. Most traditional systems lack transparency; hence, data verification can be exhausting, as well as compliance with regulations. The processes involved in patient consent and data sharing are too long. To this end, blockchain provides an immutable ledger relevant for transactions of data with the goal of making the data record transparent and traceable. On the other hand, smart contracts will automate the process of patient consent and the mode of data sharing to minimize the administrative burden to build trust, while quantum technology - or for precision QKD - is to improve the security features of block-chain to face future quantum computer attacks, compromising traditional encryption. Testing and simulation results showed that blockchain integrated with quantum technology will increase information security, reduce costs, accelerate patient recruitment, and speed up data management. Blockchain enables tamper-proof, verifiable entries, while QKD provides strong protection against future cyber threats. This dual-technology approach is, therefore, the solution to the inefficiencies and security vulnerabilities of clinical trials. The project allows further innovations in the pipeline, which quickens the delivery of new treatments to patients with security and efficiency in mind.  Β© 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Blockchain; clinical trials; data security; quantum technology},
	keywords = {Information management; Quantum efficiency; 'current; Block-chain; Clinical trial; Data Sharing; Data systems; Data tampering; Data verification; Quantum technologies; Tamperproof; Traditional systems; Blockchain},
