	affiliations = {Toronto Metropolitan University, ON, Canada; Rogers Cybersecure Catalyst, Toronto Metropolitan University, ON, Canada; Lighthouse Science Consulting and Technologies Inc., NS, Canada},
	abstract = {With the increase of IoT devices in recent times, new security solutions must be developed to protect IoT systems and the privacy of individuals and larger-scale organizations. This chapter discusses the current security approaches ranging from the hardware, software, physical, and network domains. The methodology used within this research involved the collection and analysis of several research papers that compiled current and theoretical security approaches for IoT devices. The major findings of this research were that the most common hardware approaches used to provide security in IoT devices include hardware security modules, E-LUKS, Sancus, System-on-Chip FPGA RAM Memory, and Atlas. The most common software approaches used to provide security in IoT devices include machine learning, graph theory, and blockchain. The most common network approaches used to provide security in IoT devices include secrecy capacity, channel signature/fingerprint, spectrum spreading of signal energy, and cooperation. One of the major developing trends when it comes to IoT security is the use of quantum computing through its two principles: entanglement and superposition. These two principles allow for uncompromised communication/data flow between parties as unauthorized third parties will not be able to intercept communications without changing the state of the data, allowing for all parties to be alerted of a breach. This research can be expanded on by finding more approaches related to IoT security and more trends predicted to come about in the future. Β© 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
