	affiliations = {School of Computer Science, Queensland University of Technology, Brisbane, 4000, QLD, Australia},
	abstract = {Traditional blockchains preserve everything, if verification is to avoid trusted third parties. The accumulation of outdated but undeletable data makes traditional blockchains non-scalable for practical applications. Stateless blockchains mitigate this problem via history deletion; however, existing stateless blockchains such as Mimblewimble are not quantum resistant as they rely on specific discrete-log-based cryptography to achieve history deletion. In this paper, we introduce the β€Origami Storeβ€ (or O-store for short), as a general new approach to distributed data storage with a generic history deletion mechanism called β€folding.β€ O-store is built using a hash function and an arbitrary signature scheme. Since the signature scheme is decoupled from the framework, O-stores are compatible with any quantum-resistant signature scheme by design. More importantly, O-store only needs to preserve one signature and one verification key per account (or owner) for any number of transactions (or updates), thereby mitigating the inefficiency of large quantum-safe signatures and verification keys. We prove the security of the basic O-store using the framework of Universal Composability (UC); in particular, O-storeβ€™s universal security is shown to hold for a generic consensus mechanism, to facilitate implementation from a wide range of decentralized consensus protocols. The Origami store is thus the first UC-secure efficient distributed data storage framework that supports plug-and-play consensus mechanisms for fully decentralized trustless operation. We demonstrate working implementations of Origami stores with three post-quantum signature libraries- qTESLA, Dilithium, and Picnic-to illustrate its scalability and practicality for real-world applications. Β© 2021 Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {data deletion; Decentralized storage; scalable blockchains; universal composability},
	keywords = {Authentication; Blockchain; Digital storage; Network security; Quantum cryptography; Quantum theory; Scalability; Uranium compounds; Block-chain; Data deletion; Decentralised; Decentralized storage; Distributed data storages; Scalable blockchain; Security; Signature Scheme; Universal composability; Hash functions},
